-fg);">Tetradic</div><div style="font-size:.75rem;line-height:1.3;color:var(--muted);">two complementary pairs</div></div></div><figcaption style="margin-top:.7rem;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.55;color:var(--muted);">Harmony schemes are geometry on a hue circle, not colours &mdash; so the wheel is left neutral here and only the chosen positions are filled. Twelve positions, one every 30&deg;. <b>Monochromatic</b> is the odd one out: it marks a single position and takes its variety off the hue circle altogether, moving in lightness and chroma instead, which is why its range is drawn as a column rather than a chord. The faint dashed line under <b>split complementary</b> is the true complement the scheme deliberately steps around. Because each shape is defined by angle alone, laying the same shape over a different wheel selects different hues: see the two wheels below.</figcaption></figure>
